{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793496246,"nonce":"+mNikfXG67te3x0+qWaXja0H06s=","message":"Correction to the fix described above. Accepting inside `apply_binding_deltas` was wrong: the compositor's full-paint branch calls the inner frame with a canvas-splice shortcut that goes through the same function, so a frame that asked for a full paint without emptying the cache (the frame after a programmatic scroll) reused the cache and skipped the walk. The painted set kept the off-screen spinners, `visible_anim_active` stayed true, and the window drew at vsync forever (about 15% CPU) after scrolling the spinners away.\n\nNow `apply_binding_deltas` is unchanged. Only the compositor's fast branch skips the patcher, when `RenderTree::motion_output_promoted()` holds (bindings exist, none painted outside a promoted layer, no composite binding recorded). A requested full paint walks as before.\n\nProbe (cn_demo scrolled by a ScrollRef on a timer): on the spinners every frame is fast, render step median about 0.5 ms; after scrolling to the icons or to the top, one full paint, then no frames and 0% CPU.\n\nThe test is now `promoted_rotation_beside_a_cross_stream_group_keeps_the_fast_path`: an empty overlay root puts portal groups in the dynamic batch, as in cn_demo, and a marker restyled without cache invalidation shows whether a frame walked. It fails without the shortcut (the fast frame repaints) and with the patcher-level version (the requested full paint does not walk).","files":null}]}